WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.467
-Denis, welcome to the show.

00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:03.734
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:05.234
-Thank you -- Thanks for being here.

00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:08.934
You're one of the most sought after filmmakers on the planet.

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:12.067
"Dune" was such -- I don't even know how --

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:14.033
What a beast of a movie. I don't know how --

00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:15.634
Even watching ten minutes,

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:17.234
I'm like, "How did you do any of it?"

00:00:17.234 --> 00:00:19.133
Don't tell me, but it was amazing.

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:24.033
My question is, if you weren't making movies and --

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:27.000
and brilliant movies -- what would you be doing?

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.534
What career would you be doing if it wasn't for film?

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:32.033
-Whoa. It's a good question.

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.567
I'm not as --

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:36.133
I'm not talented like Questlove, having multiple talents.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:39.033
So I think that -- One thing, I had always a plan B.

00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:40.467
-Yeah, you did?

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:43.234
-Yeah, I -- it's -- I think I could be a baker.

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:44.868
I could be a good baker.

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:47.167
I love the concept of "people need bread.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:49.934
You make bread. People buy bread."

00:00:49.934 --> 00:00:53.300
There's something very -- I love this idea.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.200
And I'm serious about it. -Really?

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.868
-Yeah, yeah. -"Do I want to be that?" Yeah.

00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:58.868
-Yeah. I want to be that guy.

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:03.300
-"Dune," as we've seen, is such a big film,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.334
and it takes so many years of work that goes into this.

00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:08.534
But what people don't know is you --

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:11.067
This took you almost 40 years, actually kind of

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.501
working on this, if you know what I'm talking about.

00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:15.467
-It's an old dream. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:17.067
I read the book when I was 13 years old.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:20.601
My best friend, Nicolas Kadima, and I, we were, like --

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:23.801
Let's say we were on the nerd side of things.

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:25.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:26.734
-The nerd side, yeah. Okay. -Yeah.

00:01:26.734 --> 00:01:30.167
And we were -- we were, like, dreaming about making movies.

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.834
We didn't have any camera, so we were drawing movies.

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:35.167
So -- And we were both in love with the book.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:38.267
And I remember doing some -- some -- Oh, here we go.

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:40.033
-Yeah, some storyboards. -Yeah.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.033
-This is when you were 13 years old.

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:43.868
-Yeah. -You drew this. Can we zoom in?

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:46.033
-I didn't -- I didn't -- Nicolas drew them.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.300
They were inspired by -- by "Dune," yeah.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.901
-Look at that. Did you use any of these shots?

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:52.567
-There -- There's some --

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:55.767
There's some one that are similar, but --

00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.767
-That -- that's insane. That's crazy.

00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:00.067
-It's an old dream. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:03.133
-I also will say -- Hans Zimmer did the score.

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.534
It's -- It's almost like it's own character in the movie.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:08.434
-Absolutely, absolutely. It's like a psychedelic dream.

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:12.200
And what he created is that he was obsessed

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.934
with the idea to create new sounds, new instruments.

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:19.400
And I know for true that he made some raids at Home Depot,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.133
buying pipes, things, and created instruments

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:25.133
to create new ways of expressing himself.

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:28.567
And -- And he -- he went back with it.

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:30.934
It created a bank of sound like that

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:33.234
and recording voices and things with voices.

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:35.133
-Because it's set in the future, and he's like, "If it's in

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:36.701
the future, we have to make a futuristic..."

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:39.067
-No violence in 10,191.

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:40.868
I mean, he wanted something

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:43.100
that was coming from another time, from another world.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.100
-Yeah, I also heard that you scored some points

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.300
because you -- you came up with bagpipes

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.968
should be in the movie somehow.

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:53.133
-Yeah, that -- It's just that there's a scene...

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:55.667
for those who saw the movie where the Atreides are landing.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.000
And I was a -- There was something missing.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.968
There was something, but I wanted to have some glimpse

00:03:00.968 --> 00:03:03.033
at the culture of the Atreides,

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:05.100
and I woke up in the middle of the night

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.501
suddenly saying, "Bagpipes."

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:10.234
And... -Wow.

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.367
-Lucky enough, we --

00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:13.968
My first assistant, Chris Carreras,

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:16.667
didn't panic and brought the bagpipe on set.

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:20.801
And then I think I gained Hans Zimmer's respect.

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:23.033
He accepted that the bagpipes

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:24.968
will exist in 10,000 years from now.

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:26.968
That was his concession.

00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:28.601
-Because it's a tricky instrument, yeah.

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:32.300
-It's -- A friend of mine, Joe Walker, we did the movie.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.000
He said, "You know what is the definition of a gentleman?

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.167
Is someone who can play the bagpipe

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:39.767
but actually don't do it."

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:42.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.968
I'm French-Canadian. -That's a good line.

00:03:43.968 --> 00:03:45.801
-Yeah, I'm French-Canadian. It's not my joke.

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:47.000
It's a British joke.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.467
-It's a great -- It's a great joke.

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:50.100
Yeah. I thought it was phenomenal.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.234
And I'm so happy that you came here.

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:54.033
We asked you if you wanted to come talk to us,

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.300
because we're big fans over here, so...

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.767
-Merci. Thank you. -Thanks so much.

00:03:56.767 --> 00:03:58.968
I want to show everyone a clip. Here's a peek at "Dune."

00:03:58.968 --> 00:04:00.467
Take a look at this.

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:08.901
♪♪

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:17.300
♪♪

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:25.734
♪♪

00:04:25.734 --> 00:04:27.400
-It's confusing.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.200
I thought I saw my death, only it wasn't.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.934
I know a knife is important. Somehow.

00:04:36.934 --> 00:04:39.634
Someone will hand me a blade,

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:43.000
but I don't know who or when or where.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.601
♪♪

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:48.167
Some things, though, are crystal clear.

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:50.534
I can feel it.

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:53.300
-Denis Villeneuve. -Merci.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.634
-"Dune" is on digital and available to stream on HBO Max.

